const scatterCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: '2*x + 1', label: 'fitted line y = 2x + 1', color: '#155e75', width: 2 }],
  points: [{
    points: [[1, 2.4], [2, 6.1], [3, 6.2], [4, 9.8], [5, 10.5], [6, 13.2], [7, 14.9], [8, 17.1], [9, 20.4], [10, 20.2]],
    label: 'observed data', mode: 'dots', color: '#b42318',
  }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 11, label: 'input x' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 24, label: 'output y' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const flowCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Question first', note: 'Decide what decision the data must inform. "Explore the data" is not a question; "which pages lose subscribers?" is.' },
    { math: '2 · Collect', note: 'Gather the evidence and note how it was produced — measurement choices are assumptions hiding inside the dataset.' },
    { math: '3 · Clean', note: 'Missing values, duplicates, impossible entries. Real cleaning takes most of the time; silent errors here poison everything downstream.' },
    { math: '4 · Explore', note: 'Plot first, model second. The scatter above is exploration doing its job: the pattern appears before any model is chosen.' },
    { math: '5 · Model & validate', note: 'Fit the simplest thing that could work, then check it on data it has not seen. Complexity must earn its keep.' },
    { math: '6 · Communicate', note: 'A result that does not change a decision was entertainment. Say what is uncertain as clearly as what is not.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/foundational/from-data-to-decisions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data science is not "statistics plus computers". It is a <strong>workflow that turns questions into
    decisions</strong> — and the definition matters because each half can fail independently. A brilliant
    analysis aimed at no decision is a museum piece; a decision made without honest evidence is a guess
    with a budget. The discipline sits at the junction of three skills: enough <em>statistics</em> to
    reason about uncertainty, enough <em>computing</em> to work with data too large for a spreadsheet,
    and enough <em>domain knowledge</em> to know which questions are worth asking and what the numbers
    actually measure. Take any one leg away and the work stops being science: without statistics it is
    dashboard decoration; without computing it cannot scale; without domain knowledge it produces
    precise answers to pointless questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The unglamorous truth is that most of the craft is plumbing and honesty: asking sharp questions,
    cleaning messy evidence, plotting before modelling, communicating uncertainty without deflating it
    into noise. The six steps below are the whole discipline in miniature, and every real project —
    from a hospital deciding which patients need follow-up calls to a streaming service deciding which
    shows to renew — walks them, usually more than once, because good analysis loops back when the data
    answers a better question than the one you asked.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="ten noisy observations, one straight answer — the line was worth fitting before anything fancier" widget="plotter" config={scatterCfg} />

  <p>
    Hover the dots: each wanders off the line by measurement noise, yet the underlying relationship is
    exactly <code>y = 2x + 1</code>. The first discipline of data science is seeing the line through the
    noise — real data never sits on the model, and demanding that it does leads to modelling the noise.
    The second discipline is admitting when there isn't a line: with different data, those same ten
    points could be pure scatter, and the professional skill is telling a real signal from wishful
    fitting. That judgement is trained, not automatic, and the rest of this lesson is the training
    course in miniature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the six-step workflow" widget="equationStep" config={flowCfg} />

  <h2>Step by step, with the traps each step hides</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>1 · Question first.</strong> Every project starts with a decision someone needs to make:
    which pages lose subscribers, which patients will miss appointments, where should the next depot
    go. A well-formed question names its decision, its deadline and its acceptable error. The famous
    historical example is John Snow and cholera (1854): the question was not "what causes cholera?" —
    too broad — but "do deaths cluster around a particular water source?" — narrow enough that a map of
    fatalities around the Broad Street pump could <em>answer</em> it. Sharp questions make data
    decisive; vague questions make data decorative.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2 · Collect.</strong> Data arrives from databases, sensors, surveys, logs — and the act of
    collection is itself a measurement instrument. If customer records only include people who finished
    signup, every conclusion silently describes finishers, not potential customers; if a survey only
    reaches people with landlines, it describes 1995. This is <strong>selection bias</strong>, and its
    signature is that the data never mentions what it is missing. The professional habit is to write
    down, before analysing, <em>how</em> each field was produced and who is absent from the file.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>3 · Clean.</strong> Practitioners estimate the majority of project time goes here, and the
    reason is structural: real data is collected by fallible systems for other purposes. Names spelled
    three ways, dates in two formats, impossible ages (230), duplicated rows, missing values that are
    coded as 0, −1, blank or the string "NULL" — each is a small lie that propagates into every
    downstream number. The rules: never clean silently (document every transformation), never clean
    toward the answer you expect, and prefer fixing the pipeline over hand-editing the symptom. A
    model trained on wrongly joined data is confidently wrong forever.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>4 · Explore.</strong> Plot first, model second. Histograms reveal skew and multimodality;
    scatter plots reveal relationships; box plots reveal outliers; summary tables reveal impossible
    values. This is <strong>exploratory data analysis</strong>, formalised by John Tukey (1962), and its
    philosophy inverts the classroom: do not fit a model and check it against the data — look at the
    data until it tells you which model would be honest. The scatter above is EDA doing its job: before
    anyone proposes a neural network, ten dots and a straight line have already settled the first
    question. Exploration also calibrates scepticism: Anscombe's quartet (1973) shows four datasets
    with <em>identical</em> means, variances, correlations and regression lines — and wildly different
    pictures. Plotting is not presentation; it is inspection.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>5 · Model &amp; validate.</strong> Fit the simplest thing that could work — often a
    baseline ("predict the average", "predict yesterday's value") — then something slightly smarter,
    and only promote complexity that beats the baseline on data it has <em>not seen</em>. That last
    clause is the entire safeguard against the field's central disease, <strong>overfitting</strong>: a
    flexible model can memorise the noise in its training data, scoring beautifully there and
    embarrassingly on new data. The defence is to hold out a test set (or cross-validate) and report
    performance only there. Complexity must earn its keep on unseen data; a parameter that improves
    training fit but not test fit is a lie the model tells itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>6 · Communicate.</strong> A result that does not change a decision was entertainment. The
    deliverables are a number with its uncertainty ("renewal likely lifts retention by 2–4%, we're 90%
    confident it's positive"), a chart honest enough to survive scrutiny (axes from zero, baselines
    shown), and a recommendation with its assumptions stated. Uncertainty is content, not weakness:
    a forecast delivered without a range invites decisions the evidence cannot support.
  </p>

  <h2>A case end to end: why do subscribers leave?</h2>
  <p>
    Watch the six steps carry one real decision. <strong>Question</strong>: a streaming service is
    losing subscribers; the finance team wants to know whether to spend a fixed retention budget on
    discounts or on content. Sharpened: <em>which members are likely to cancel next month, and is
    there a lever we control?</em> <strong>Collect</strong>: eighteen months of account records —
    signup date, plan, viewing hours per week, devices used, payment history, and the flag that
    matters: who actually cancelled. Note the instrument immediately: the file contains only people
    who <em>signed up</em> (selection: it says nothing about those who bounced off the landing page)
    and viewing hours are tracked only when the app is open (measurement: hours near zero could mean
    dissatisfaction <em>or</em> a holiday). <strong>Clean</strong>: duplicates from a billing-system
    migration are removed; "hours per week" for a fortnight with a two-week outage is recoded as
    missing rather than zero; the eleven accounts with negative tenure are dropped and the pipeline
    bug reported. Every transformation is logged.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Explore</strong>: the cancellations are not uniform — churn spikes in month three
    (the free-trial cliff), among members whose viewing fell by more than half month-over-month, and
    on the ad-supported plan. A scatter of viewing-hours against tenure shows the falling-engagement
    pattern plainly, and a box plot shows churners' engagement dropping weeks <em>before</em> they
    cancel — the signal a retention offer could intercept. <strong>Model &amp; validate</strong>: the
    baseline ("everyone renews") is useless for targeting; a simple model — flag members with
    declining hours in their second month — is tested on the six months it never saw and catches
    members at several times the base rate of churn, which is the number that matters (you cannot
    save everyone; you need a list worth calling). A fancier model is tried and rejected: it beats
    the simple one on training data but not on held-out data — complexity did not earn its keep.
    <strong>Communicate</strong>: "Members whose viewing drops by half after month one cancel at
    several times the average rate; an email offering a free month to that group is projected to
    retain one in eight of them at a cost well below a month's subscription — we recommend a
    two-market trial with a control group, and we'll know in six weeks whether to scale." Numbers,
    uncertainty, a controlled test, a decision. That is the workflow doing exactly what it was
    built for.
  </p>

  <h2>What separates professionals</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the workflow, four habits mark the difference between analysis and theatre.
    <strong>Baselines first</strong> — a model that cannot beat "guess the average" is not a model, and
    running the dumb baseline first costs minutes and saves careers. <strong>The dataset is an
    instrument</strong> — ask what it measures, in what population, with what gaps, before asking what
    it says. <strong>Correlation still is not causation</strong> — ice-cream sales and drownings rise
    together because of summer, not dessert; dashboards make it easier than ever to imply causation by
    arrangement alone, and the honest tools (randomised experiments, natural experiments, careful
    adjustment) are deliberate acts, not side effects of a clever chart. The famous cautionary tale is
    Google Flu Trends (2008): it predicted flu from search terms brilliantly — then confidently
    over-predicted for years as its own recommendations changed what people searched, a lesson in how
    the instrument changes the thing measured. <strong>Reproducibility is trust</strong> — a result
    nobody can re-run from the raw data and code is an anecdote with charts; versioned code, recorded
    data lineage and saved random seeds are what make a finding an asset rather than a story.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> data science is not mostly machine learning, and "the data will
  speak for itself" is the field's most dangerous slogan. Data speaks only through the question asked,
  the way it was gathered and the model imposed on it — all human choices. The best data scientists
  spend most of their effort on steps 1–3 and 6, which is precisely where the judgement lives.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers lie beneath the workflow. <strong>The experimental layer</strong>: the gold standard
    for turning correlation into causation is the randomised controlled trial — randomly assign the new
    feature to half the users, compare outcomes. Randomisation balances every confounder, known and
    unknown, which is why A/B testing is the workhorse of internet decision-making; when experiments
    are impossible (you cannot randomise smoking), statistics offers quasi-experimental tools —
    difference-in-differences, regression discontinuity — each with stated assumptions. <strong>The
    statistical layer</strong>: every estimate has a distribution, and aggregates can reverse at the
    subgroup level (Simpson's paradox — the Berkeley admissions case is the canonical example), so
    professional practice reports uncertainty intervals and interrogates subgroups before believing
    totals. <strong>The human layer</strong>: data about people is data about people — privacy,
    consent and bias in, bias out. A hiring model trained on past hires learns the past's
    discriminations as signal; a credit model can encode geography as a proxy for race. This is why
    modern practice treats ethics as an engineering requirement — audits, fairness metrics, documented
    datasets — rather than a press-release afterthought. The future of the field is argued over
    (automated AutoML pipelines vs craftsman analysis), but the six steps have been stable for decades
    precisely because they encode something machines do not supply by default: the discipline of being
    honest with yourself in public.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tukey, "The Future of Data Analysis" (1962); Anscombe (1973); Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera (1855); Lazer et al., "The Parable of Google Flu" (2014); Wickham & Grolemund, R for Data Science.</p>
</TopicPage>
